        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 529 ---\n494\nMASUREMENT OF RESISTANCE.\nwhere $R_{2}$ is the resistance between $A_{2}$ and $B_{2}$. If the observa-\ntions of the galvanometers $G_{1}$ and $G_{2}$ are simultaneous, the\nvalue of $C$, the current in the primary circuit, is the same in\nboth equations, and we find\n$$E_{1}: E_{2}:: R_{1}: R_{2} .$$\nIn this way the electromotive forces of two electromotors may\nbe compared. The absolute electromotive force of an electro-\nmotor may be measured either electrostatically by means of\nthe electrometer, or eiectromagnetically by means of an absolute\ngalvanometer.\nThis method, in which, at the time of the comparison, there\nis no current through either of the electromotors, is a modi-\nfication of Poggendorff's method, and is due to Mr. Latimer\nClark, who has deduced the following values of electromotive\nforces:\n\\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|l|l|l|}\n\\hline & & & Concentrated solution of & & Volts. \\\\\n\\hline Daniell I. & Amalgamated Zinc & $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+4$ aq. & $\\mathrm{Cu} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ & Copper & $=1.079$ \\\\\n\\hline II. & \" & $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+12 \\mathrm{aq}$. & $\\mathrm{Cu} \\mathrm{SO}{ }_{4}$ & Copper & $=0.978$ \\\\\n\\hline III. & \" & $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+12$ aq. & $\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{\\mathrm{g}}\\right)_{2}$ & Copper & $=1.00$ \\\\\n\\hline Bunsen & \" & \" \" & $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ & Carbon & $=1.964$ \\\\\n\\hline II. & & \" & p. g. 1.38 & Carbon & $=1.888$ \\\\\n\\hline Grove & \" & $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+{ }^{\\prime \\prime} 4$ aq. & $\\mathrm{H} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ & Platinum & $=1.956$ \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nConcentrated solution of\nConcentrated\nsolution of\nVolts.\nDaniell I.\nAmalgamated Zinc\n$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+4$ aq.\n$\\mathrm{Cu} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$\nCopper\n$=1.079$\nII.\n\"\n$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+12 \\mathrm{aq}$.\n$\\mathrm{Cu} \\mathrm{SO}{ }_{4}$\nCopper\n$=0.978$\nIII.\n\"\n$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+12$ aq.\n$\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{\\mathrm{g}}\\right)_{2}$\nCopper\n$=1.00$\nBunsen\n\"\n\" \"\n$\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$\nCarbon\n$=1.964$\nII.\n\"\np. g. 1.38\nCarbon\n$=1.888$\nGrove\n\"\n$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+{ }^{\\prime \\prime} 4$ aq.\n$\\mathrm{H} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}$\nPlatinum\n$=1.956$\nA Volt is an electromotice force equal to $100,000,00$ units of the centimetre-\ngramme-second system.\n",
